      "side": 1,
      "message": "To guarantee that `file.read(1)` in `readline()` will never block, the underlying socket or PTY file descriptor must be explicitly set to non-blocking mode. Otherwise, if `select.select()` experiences a spurious wakeup (which can happen on Linux, especially with sockets), `file.read(1)` could still block indefinitely, defeating the purpose of this CL.\n\nYou can set the file descriptor to non-blocking right after it\u0027s opened. Note that doing this will cause `file.read(1)` to return `None` on empty reads, making the fix in `readline()` mandatory.\n\n```python\n        if args.remote and args.console_port:\n            console_socket \u003d stack.enter_context(\n                socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n            )\n            console_socket.connect((args.remote, args.console_port))\n            console_socket.setblocking(False)  # Ensure non-blocking\n            console \u003d stack.enter_context(\n                console_socket.makefile(mode\u003d\"rwb\", buffering\u003d0)\n            )\n        else:\n            console_pty \u003d platform.get_console(board_config)\n            if not console_pty:\n                logging.error(\"Unable to find console pty\")\n                return False\n            # pylint: disable-next\u003dconsider-using-with\n            console \u003d stack.enter_context(\n                open(console_pty, \"r+b\", buffering\u003d0)\n            )\n            os.set_blocking(console.fileno(), False)  # Ensure non-blocking\n```",
